- Retrieved practices:
- Do not access objects outside their lifetime
WHAT: Accessing objects (including temporaries) after their lifetime ends, such as via dangling references or pointers, causes undefined behavior.
WHY: Using invalid objects can lead to memory corruption, crashes, data leaks, or exploitable vulnerabilities.
HOW: Avoid storing references to temporaries; ensure referenced objects outlive all uses; prefer owning containers (e.g., std::vector instead of std::initializer_list references) and avoid returning const references to values created as temporaries.
EXAMPLE (C++ before): std::function<const int&(const int&)> fn = [](const int &j){ return j; }; int j = fn(i); // returns ref to temporary
EXAMPLE (C++ after): std::function<int(const int&)> fn = [](const int &j){ return j; }; int j = fn(i); // returns by value, no dangling reference

- Avoid information leakage when copying C++ objects across trust boundaries
WHAT: Copying a C++ object’s raw memory (including padding bytes) to a less-trusted context (e.g., user space) can leak uninitialized or stale register data via padding bytes.
WHY: Leaked padding bytes may contain sensitive information from previous computations, leading to unintended data disclosure.
HOW: Do not use raw memory copies (e.g., memcpy, copy_to_user with sizeof(object)) on non-trivially copyable objects; instead, copy only explicitly initialized fields into a dedicated POD/struct with no padding or serialize fields individually.
EXAMPLE: Before (unsafe): copy_to_user(usr_buf, &arg, sizeof(arg)); After (safe): struct dto { int a; char b; int c; } out{arg.a, arg.b, arg.c}; copy_to_user(usr_buf, &out, sizeof(out));

- Proper deallocation of dynamically allocated resources
WHAT: Misusing delete (e.g., calling delete instead of delete[] for arrays, or bypassing class-specific operator delete) causes undefined behavior and resource mismanagement.
WHY: Incorrect deallocation can lead to memory corruption, leaks, and exploitable crashes.
HOW: Always match new with delete and new[] with delete[]; for smart pointers, use the correct pointer type (e.g., std::unique_ptr<T[]> for arrays) or provide an appropriate custom deleter so the correct deallocation function is invoked.
EXAMPLE: Before (noncompliant, array with scalar deleter): std::unique_ptr<S> p{new S[10]}; After (compliant): std::unique_ptr<S[]> p{new S[10]};

- Do not leak resources when handling exceptions
WHAT: Failing to release dynamically allocated resources when an exception is thrown (for example, returning or rethrowing before delete/free) causes resource leaks.
WHY: Resource leaks can exhaust memory or other resources, leading to denial of service, instability, or degraded performance.
HOW: Use RAII (resource acquisition is initialization) objects (e.g., smart pointers, wrapper classes) so that destructors automatically release resources in all control paths, including exceptions; avoid raw new/delete in code that can throw.
EXAMPLE: Before (leaks on exception): `T* p = new T(); risky(); delete p;` After (no leak): `std::unique_ptr<T> p = std::make_unique<T>(); risky();`

- Guarantee sufficient storage for strings and null terminators
WHAT: Copying or reading strings into a buffer that is too small, or that does not reserve space for the null terminator, can cause buffer overflows (for example, using `std::cin >> buf` where `buf` is a fixed-size `char` array).
WHY: Buffer overflows can corrupt memory, cause crashes, and enable code execution vulnerabilities.
HOW: Always ensure the destination buffer is large enough for the maximum expected data plus the null terminator; when using input APIs, explicitly bound the number of characters read to `buffer_size - 1` and do not rely on stateful width settings that reset after use.
EXAMPLE: Before (unsafe, may overflow): `char buf[12]; std::cin >> buf;` After (safer): `char buf[12]; std::cin.width(sizeof(buf)); std::cin >> std::setw(sizeof(buf)) >> buf;`

- Do not access freed memory
WHAT: Accessing memory after it has been freed or gone out of scope (dangling pointers) leads to undefined behavior and potential security vulnerabilities.
WHY: Use-after-free can cause crashes, data corruption, or be exploited for arbitrary code execution.
HOW: Ensure object lifetimes cover all uses; prefer RAII types like std::unique_ptr or std::string over raw heap buffers, and avoid manual delete/free where possible.
EXAMPLE: Before (unsafe): `char *buff = new char[32]; delete[] buff; std::cout << buff;` After (safe): `std::string str = argv[1]; std::cout << str;`



